n-trivial & 26 & \\ref{b:nontrivial}\\\\\nunique vacuum & 27 & \\ref{b:vacuum}\\\\\nmass gap $\\Delta>0$ & 20, 28 & \\ref{b:h3}, \\ref{b:lemma}, \\ref{b:deduce}\\\\\nit is Yang--Mills & 1, 14, 21 & \\ref{b:wilson}, \\ref{b:b0}, \\ref{b:box}\\\\\n\\bottomrule\\end{tabular}\\end{minipage}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\n\\textbf{(e) What this is.} A complete narrative theory of the Yang--Mills mass gap: complete, because every clause has a\nline and every line a place in the story; unproven, because H3, H4$\'$, H6 and H7 are conjectures, and H3 is the wall\nitself; falsifiable, because each claim has a place where the geometry can tell me no. What I offer here is the shape of the answer: the gap is the lowest note of a\nfield that cannot change its topology for free, and it is the same note in every room.\n'

